# The delimiter-scan WINDOW-SAFETY premise pin.
#
# WHAT THIS FIXTURE PINS (Core #12 — the cell, not a vague claim): the
# `)`-scan that derives a parameter list's gate end starts at the LAST
# PARAMETER'S SPAN END, and `Param.span` INCLUDES the default-value
# expression. Were the premise false — were the span to end at the
# parameter NAME — the scan would start inside `"a)b"` and return the `)`
# that lives inside the STRING LITERAL, truncating the gate's container
# end to a byte before the real `)`.
#
# WHY THE COMMENT SITS WHERE IT DOES: a truncated container end is
# INVISIBLE to an ordinary mid-list interior comment (that comment is
# inside the window either way, so the gate still fires and the list still
# breaks). It is observable only through the ORPHAN-PRE-CLOSE flush, which
# `format_bracketed_broken_with_comments` runs against the container end
# itself. So the sentinel is an own-line comment AFTER the last parameter
# and BEFORE the `)`.
#
# TWO REDS, both recorded in the round's report:
#   1. Ordinary pre-fix red: the whole parameter list was ungated, so the
#      sentinel re-parented INTO the function body and documented the
#      first body statement (`print(y)`).
#   2. Simulated premise-break red (the one that pins the premise): with
#      the last parameter's span end forced to the parameter name's end,
#      the `)`-scan lands inside `"a)b"` and the sentinel escapes again.
#
# The string literal doubles as the adversarial probe for the scan itself:
# a `)` inside a string must never be mistaken for a delimiter.

void f(
    int y,
    String s = "a)b",
    # SEN window-orphan-pre-close
):
    print(y)
    print(s)

int main():
    f(1)
    return 0
